(1496990, 194058, 38941, '<td valign="top">very very very good but u did some things which f***ed it up for those nasty judges.<br/>1.her hands should have been centered<br/>2.u should have used neat image for that noise<br/>3.higher contrast to make it more intense.<br/> <br/>I learned this by my mistakes on dpc. Anyway a magnificent shot. or semi :P<br/></td>', 'very very very good but u did some things which f***ed it up for those nasty judges.1.her hands should have been centered2.u should have used neat image for that noise3.higher contrast to make it more intense. I learned this by my mistakes on dpc. Anyway a magnificent shot. or semi :P', '2005-07-01 07:42:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(1497375, 192604, 22379, '<td valign="top">Denise, i wasn''t the biggest fan of this image, i gave a 4. While it is in focus, crisp and evenly lit, it lacked the personal flavour of the individual photographer that i tend to see in images i vote higher. <br/>My first impression, based on the composition and angle of this photo was that you walked past the pump, realised it was appropriate for the metal challenge and turned around to take a snap. I wondered why you chose to take the photo at this angle, why you decided to cut the pump off where you did, why you took it at this level/perspective - and from the picture alone these questions were not answered. <br/>An old rusted pump like this has seen some stuff in his days - he has a story to tell, and i felt you missed the opportunity to capitalise on the intrigue of the subject matter. <br/>You technical aspect ids excellent - but don''t be afraid to put a little bit more of yourself into your work!</td>', 'Denise, i wasn''t the biggest fan of this image, i gave a 4. While it is in focus, crisp and evenly lit, it lacked the personal flavour of the individual photographer that i tend to see in images i vote higher. My first impression, based on the composition and angle of this photo was that you walked past the pump, realised it was appropriate for the metal challenge and turned around to take a snap. I wondered why you chose to take the photo at this angle, why you decided to cut the pump off where you did, why you took it at this level/perspective - and from the picture alone these questions were not answered. An old rusted pump like this has seen some stuff in his days - he has a story to tell, and i felt you missed the opportunity to capitalise on the intrigue of the subject matter. You technical aspect ids excellent - but don''t be afraid to put a little bit more of yourself into your work!', '2005-07-01 12:59:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(1497390, 193894, 22379, '<td valign="top">Kevin - in all honesty, when i first saw this photo (as a thumb), i didn''t like it much, then in full view i was 50/50 and as the challenge went on it grew on me every time i saw it eventually ending up as a 7 haha. As others have said, the lighting is good, as is the DOF - but it did take me a little while to get over the initial  consideration of  the DOF that you chose. <br/>I like the frame/crop a lot, i feel the elements are nicely balanced. <br/>I would have liked to see this in black and white - the properties of the object lead them selves so readily to be silver-grey rather than have an orange tinge imho. Also, a whiter white and an blacker shadow would have given the image a bit for "wow" factor for me. <br/>I don''t know why someone linked you to the size faq, it was completely irrelevent for this entry - must have had tier wires crossed!<br/>Definately  an image to be viewed more than once!<br/></td>', 'Kevin - in all honesty, when i first saw this photo (as a thumb), i didn''t like it much, then in full view i was 50/50 and as the challenge went on it grew on me every time i saw it eventually ending up as a 7 haha. As others have said, the lighting is good, as is the DOF - but it did take me a little while to get over the initial  consideration of  the DOF that you chose. I like the frame/crop a lot, i feel the elements are nicely balanced. I would have liked to see this in black and white - the properties of the object lead them selves so readily to be silver-grey rather than have an orange tinge imho. Also, a whiter white and an blacker shadow would have given the image a bit for "wow" factor for me. I don''t know why someone linked you to the size faq, it was completely irrelevent for this entry - must have had tier wires crossed!Definately  an image to be viewed more than once!', '2005-07-01 13:13:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(1497473, 192745, 22379, '<td valign="top">Well i wrote you a long comment - and it was deleted. How fun.<br/>Chesire, i gave a 4. FIrst off let me say how glad i am that you used an ironed sheet as a backdrop. Entirely too often i see crinkled old bedclothes used, and it just isnt pretty. So Thankyou!<br/>The concept of the photo is beautiful, and it is well composed. However the first thing that caught my eye in this photo was the smutz on babies ring finger. I wished you had wiped her little hands off. And maybe a little baby lotion to smooth down the horny skin on her index finger.<br/>As you probably realised, 60 watts isnt much light for a camera. With a bit more lighting, the image would have been crisper. I was quite surprised when i checked your aperture that this was shot at f8. I would have used a macro setting to take this photo, to capture the finer details. <br/> <br/>For your questions: Since we are now out of the challenage, to improve, i would use the heal brush to remove the problems spots from babies hands. I would use curves (carefully) to make the sheet a more soild black ( avoiding making the hand orangey). I might also experiment to see how the image looked as black and white by using the desaturate (not the grayscale) option.<br/>For copyright info - read the post i made yesterday here: //www.dpchallenge.com/forum.php?action=read&amp;FORUM_THREAD_ID=234239<br/>You can make yourself a symbol or just write your ©year name on one of the corners of the file if you like - however no copyright info added to an image is admissable fro challenge photos (you can have it on your portfolio stuff).   <br/></td>', 'Well i wrote you a long comment - and it was deleted. How fun.Chesire, i gave a 4. FIrst off let me say how glad i am that you used an ironed sheet as a backdrop. Entirely too often i see crinkled old bedclothes used, and it just isnt pretty. So Thankyou!The concept of the photo is beautiful, and it is well composed. However the first thing that caught my eye in this photo was the smutz on babies ring finger. I wished you had wiped her little hands off. And maybe a little baby lotion to smooth down the horny skin on her index finger.As you probably realised, 60 watts isnt much light for a camera. With a bit more lighting, the image would have been crisper. I was quite surprised when i checked your aperture that this was shot at f8. I would have used a macro setting to take this photo, to capture the finer details.  For your questions: Since we are now out of the challenage, to improve, i would use the heal brush to remove the problems spots from babies hands. I would use curves (carefully) to make the sheet a more soild black ( avoiding making the hand orangey). I might also experiment to see how the image looked as black and white by using the desaturate (not the grayscale) option.For copyright info - read the post i made yesterday here: //www.dpchallenge.com/forum.php?action=read&FORUM_THREAD_ID=234239You can make yourself a symbol or just write your ©year name on one of the corners of the file if you like - however no copyright info added to an image is admissable fro challenge photos (you can have it on your portfolio stuff).   ', '2005-07-01 14:02:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(1497559, 191319, 22379, '<td valign="top">Thomas - you are not going to like me much, but i gave a 4. This image just didn''t "do it" for me. you came 47/501 so it obviously did it for a lot of people, just not me.<br/>But, here is why anyhow - I didn''t care for the perspective you chose for this subject matter, the clash of tried and true vs overdone comes to mind. Po Boxes, lockers etc they are always photographed this way. What if you had veered a little left of the norm and made the lines run diagonal? <br/>There is plenty of light - in fact some boxes are a little blown out - and with so much light, i would have expected a little sharper focus. No particular box stands out as the one that is most in focus for me. <br/>Now the crop, the crop is very good, nice and symetrically balanced through the image. That i did like.</td>', 'Thomas - you are not going to like me much, but i gave a 4. This image just didn''t "do it" for me. you came 47/501 so it obviously did it for a lot of people, just not me.But, here is why anyhow - I didn''t care for the perspective you chose for this subject matter, the clash of tried and true vs overdone comes to mind. Po Boxes, lockers etc they are always photographed this way. What if you had veered a little left of the norm and made the lines run diagonal? There is plenty of light - in fact some boxes are a little blown out - and with so much light, i would have expected a little sharper focus. No particular box stands out as the one that is most in focus for me. Now the crop, the crop is very good, nice and symetrically balanced through the image. That i did like.', '2005-07-01 14:37:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(1497589, 192917, 22379, '<td valign="top">Caltrop  - i gave a 4. I am personally not a fan of photographs of sculptures. I think you understand my hesitation, because of what you have stated in your comment.<br/>The sky is Wonderful. Perfect in every way - i would have liked to have seen more of it - as in the photo taken in rule of thumb style - the heads in the left  third and mostly sky in the other two, to give the indication that the giraffes are on the move. Right now the crop is too tight for my likelings which is why it feel into the "picture of a scuplture" rather that "Caltrops picture of metal giraffes on the run" or some such interpretation of the sculpture for me. <br/>I have a feeling you could have upped the saturation on the red &amp; yellow, to give the giraffes a bit more depth. With the brilliant blue<br/>sky, i would have liked to have seen brilliantly coloured giraffes to match. <br/>It would be interesting to see this shot taken at different times of day, to see how the suns relative position efected the apperance of the trio. Might want to check that out?</td>', 'Caltrop  - i gave a 4. I am personally not a fan of photographs of sculptures. I think you understand my hesitation, because of what you have stated in your comment.The sky is Wonderful. Perfect in every way - i would have liked to have seen more of it - as in the photo taken in rule of thumb style - the heads in the left  third and mostly sky in the other two, to give the indication that the giraffes are on the move. Right now the crop is too tight for my likelings which is why it feel into the "picture of a scuplture" rather that "Caltrops picture of metal giraffes on the run" or some such interpretation of the sculpture for me. I have a feeling you could have upped the saturation on the red & yellow, to give the giraffes a bit more depth. With the brilliant bluesky, i would have liked to have seen brilliantly coloured giraffes to match. It would be interesting to see this shot taken at different times of day, to see how the suns relative position efected the apperance of the trio. Might want to check that out?', '2005-07-01 14:50:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(1498235, 192357, 34659, '<td valign="top">I really like this shot - and I like the fact that the building''s clock is "inside" the sculpture.  Beautiful green colors, slightly overexposed, but a great shot!</td>', 'I really like this shot - and I like the fact that the building''s clock is "inside" the sculpture.  Beautiful green colors, slightly overexposed, but a great shot!', '2005-07-01 19:36:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(1499155, 191422, 39243, '<td valign="top">well... I was a carpenter for many years and even though i recognize the difficulty in handcrafting something this shot to me is unappealing. The light is a little harsh and the shadow on the hand make the hand ehhh I am not sure how to put this but.... i guess too dark.?   Finally the theme was metal and the image is primarily composed of wood and flesh with the metal dull and unpronounced. Oh yeah one more thing the chisel you are using seems a wee bit on the large size to be carving a man shape.<br/> <br/>I hope this helps a wee bit- sorry if that was harsh.</td>', 'well... I was a carpenter for many years and even though i recognize the difficulty in handcrafting something this shot to me is unappealing. The light is a little harsh and the shadow on the hand make the hand ehhh I am not sure how to put this but.... i guess too dark.?   Finally the theme was metal and the image is primarily composed of wood and flesh with the metal dull and unpronounced. Oh yeah one more thing the chisel you are using seems a wee bit on the large size to be carving a man shape. I hope this helps a wee bit- sorry if that was harsh.', '2005-07-02 04:43:56.000000', NULL, 0),
